1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,240
It is known as technology's angel of death, but it might be humanity's savior, able to

2
00:00:08,241 --> 00:00:12,200
generate a nearly limitless supply of power.

3
00:00:12,201 --> 00:00:16,760
Technology sired by splitting the atom has led to an incredible array of innovations.

4
00:00:16,761 --> 00:00:20,640
Now, Nuclear Tech on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,400
3, 2, 1, and liftoff of the Cassini spacecraft

6
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,540
on a trillion-mile trek to Saturn.

7
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:43,080
When the Cassini spacecraft launched in 1997,

8
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:47,220
its goal was to journey to Saturn, about 890 million miles

9
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:48,660
from Earth.

10
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:51,760
But providing power for such a long journey called

11
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:53,720
for inventive measures.

12
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:56,200
Conventional chemical propulsion would make the craft

13
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:57,400
too heavy.

14
00:00:57,401 --> 00:01:00,060
and because Saturn is so far from the sun,

15
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:03,840
solar power would be ineffective.

16
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:07,480
But there was another choice, nuclear technology,

17
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:12,440
in the form of a radioisotope thermoelectric generator.

18
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:15,060
The radioisotope thermoelectric generator

19
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:19,460
produces electricity continuously over a long period

20
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:21,420
of time, many decades.

21
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:24,420
And it produces it in the absence of sunlight

22
00:01:24,421 --> 00:01:28,260
sunlight and is a wonderful, stable source of electricity

23
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:31,520
to keep our spacecraft running in the cool, dark heaps of space,

24
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:34,060
basically.

25
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:37,160
The Cassini spacecraft carries three radioisotope

26
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:39,760
thermoelectric generator units.

27
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:44,080
These RTGs take advantage of an isotopic decay process.

28
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:48,880
Isotopes over time decay by releasing alpha particles.

29
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:52,120
The process also gives off energy in the form of heat.

30
00:01:52,121 --> 00:01:55,860
When this happens, matter is converted to energy.

31
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:59,600
It's Einstein's famous equation, E equals mc squared,

32
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:01,100
come to life.

33
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:04,600
Energy released equals the mass that is converted to energy

34
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:08,100
times the speed of light squared.

35
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,100
Cassini's thermoelectric modules

36
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:12,580
convert the heat released to electricity.

37
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:17,120
In the event of a mechanical failure

38
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:20,880
during launch preparation, at launch, or in space,

39
00:02:20,881 --> 00:02:23,780
RTG designers have embedded the plutonium fuel

40
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:27,280
in a heat-resistant ceramic so the radioactive material

41
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:29,920
won't disperse, even if the reentry capsule

42
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:32,420
were to break apart.

43
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:35,120
So that if it should get free, it

44
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:38,500
would go through the atmosphere and come back down

45
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:42,960
to the Earth without burning up or breaking up.

46
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:46,980
The level of scrutiny and safety and design that's

47
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:50,880
employed in these things is truly extraordinary.

48
00:02:50,881 --> 00:02:55,080
The Cassini spacecraft is the latest to use radioisotope

49
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:56,620
thermoelectric generators.

50
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:01,120
And that's providing all the power we need

51
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:02,120
for the whole spacecraft.

52
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:06,420
Encouraged by the success of RTGs,

53
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:09,200
NASA is planning a long-range nuclear-powered mission

54
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:11,700
to orbit Jupiter's three moons.

55
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:14,740
It will use a propulsion system called Prometheus,

56
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:18,140
augmented by a solar power array.

57
00:03:18,141 --> 00:03:24,820
Instead of producing 500 watts of electricity, which we get out of the isotope power sources,

58
00:03:24,821 --> 00:03:30,580
the Prometheus power systems are designed to deliver something in the order of 100 kilowatts,

59
00:03:30,581 --> 00:03:33,740
enough to light up a small football field.

60
00:03:33,741 --> 00:03:39,720
These reactor systems and the ion drives provide a steady source of thrust, which is a rocket

61
00:03:39,721 --> 00:03:40,900
scientist's dream.

62
00:03:40,901 --> 00:03:45,900
Scientists have also found that nuclear technology can fulfill

63
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,380
their dreams in the area of medical treatment.

64
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:53,940
Doctors use nuclear imaging to read the emotions of the brain.

65
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:57,220
Cancer treatment has improved with the use of radioactive

66
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:00,860
isotopes to attack tumor cells.

67
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:04,760
Food and medical instruments can be sanitized using nuclear

68
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:08,300
irradiation without removing the radiation.

69
00:04:08,301 --> 00:04:11,200
without removing them from their packaging.

70
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:13,540
Scientists are also using nuclear technology

71
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:15,780
to battle terrorism and search for ways

72
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:17,880
to provide abundant electrical power

73
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:20,280
without harm to the environment.

74
00:04:23,420 --> 00:04:25,820
But while things nuclear may be lighting the way in medicine

75
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:28,620
and in academia, it's still a technology

76
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:30,120
with a dark history.

77
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:38,300
It began at a New Mexico lab called Los Alamos.

78
00:04:38,301 --> 00:04:42,300
and it was a race to make the first nuclear bomb.

79
00:04:42,301 --> 00:04:44,300
Scientists answered an urgent summons

80
00:04:44,301 --> 00:04:47,300
to be part of the Manhattan Project.

81
00:04:47,301 --> 00:04:50,300
The main inspiration for getting it going

82
00:04:50,301 --> 00:04:52,300
was a fear of a German bomb.

83
00:04:52,301 --> 00:04:57,300
This was in the late 1930s when fission was just discovered

84
00:04:57,301 --> 00:05:00,300
and war had just broken out in Europe.

85
00:05:00,301 --> 00:05:05,300
And I heard a talk by President Roosevelt,

86
00:05:05,301 --> 00:05:08,240
a talk to scientists,

87
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:13,540
telling us Hitler was a very real danger to the whole world,

88
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:18,860
and the oceans were no longer necessarily a sufficient protection.

89
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:21,240
And then he said,

90
00:05:22,860 --> 00:05:27,540
you scientists are accused of making the means

91
00:05:27,541 --> 00:05:31,980
by which destruction can be accomplished.

92
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:34,620
But I have to tell you,

93
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:42,300
Unless you will work on weapons, Hitler will win.

94
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:47,780
From 1939 until 1945, more than $2 billion,

95
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:51,880
$1945, were spent on the Manhattan Project.

96
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:54,080
It attracted the best and the brightest.

97
00:05:54,180 --> 00:05:56,120
There were eight Nobel Prize winners already

98
00:05:56,220 --> 00:05:59,200
on the Manhattan Project, and more than a dozen others

99
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:01,320
would win prizes after the war.

100
00:06:01,321 --> 00:06:04,660
So this is a concentration of scientific talent

101
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:07,100
that's perhaps unequal.

102
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:09,260
All that brain power and willpower

103
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:12,300
was needed to overcome the daunting technical challenges

104
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:16,340
of creating America's first nuclear weapon.

105
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:19,380
Manhattan Project scientists wanted to generate what would

106
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:22,440
be known as a fission reaction.

107
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:27,340
To accomplish that, they needed an isotope of uranium.

108
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:30,520
Like all elements, uranium is made up of atoms

109
00:06:30,521 --> 00:06:33,520
and the atoms themselves are made up of smaller parts,

110
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:37,520
a central nucleus that's comprised of protons,

111
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:43,520
neutrons, and electrons.

112
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:47,520
Naturally occurring uranium is called U-238 because its

113
00:06:47,620 --> 00:06:51,520
protons and neutrons total 238.

114
00:06:51,620 --> 00:06:55,520
It also contains a small accumulation of U-235,

115
00:06:55,620 --> 00:06:59,520
a form of uranium that has only 143 neutrons.

116
00:06:59,521 --> 00:07:04,660
As the Manhattan Project scientists knew,

117
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:07,920
uranium-235 was needed to make their bomb,

118
00:07:08,020 --> 00:07:12,400
because it can undergo what is known as a chain reaction.

119
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:16,100
A chain reaction occurs if each time a neutron is released,

120
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:20,100
it produces another fission reaction, which in turn

121
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:22,480
releases more neutrons.

122
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:27,580
With each fission, more matter is converted into energy.

123
00:07:27,581 --> 00:07:31,180
If the U-235 is concentrated properly within a millionth

124
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:33,760
of a second, enough fission reactions occur

125
00:07:33,860 --> 00:07:37,960
to release the power of an atomic bomb.

126
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:40,720
To keep the chain reaction from starting prematurely,

127
00:07:40,820 --> 00:07:45,240
Los Alamos scientists kept the uranium in two separate masses.

128
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:49,640
At the time of detonation, they would be brought together.

129
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:53,900
They believed one way to do this was to make a gun that fired

130
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:55,640
one mass into another.

131
00:07:55,641 --> 00:07:58,280
They were separated by a long tube,

132
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:01,280
and then you fired some conventional explosive,

133
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:04,380
and the two masses joined together,

134
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,920
and you had a fission reaction.

135
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:11,760
This method of ignition is called gun-triggered fission.

136
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:14,920
Los Alamos scientists were also working on a more complex

137
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:19,360
and far more powerful device, an implosion-triggered bomb.

138
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:22,360
Nicknamed the gadget, it used high explosives

139
00:08:22,361 --> 00:08:26,840
explosives to compress uranium-235 around a spherical core

140
00:08:26,940 --> 00:08:29,100
of plutonium-239.

141
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:32,240
When they combined, the fission reaction began.

142
00:08:32,340 --> 00:08:36,840
The plutonium bomb had wires in it and detonators,

143
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:40,140
and they all fired at exactly the precise time

144
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:43,240
and drove this high explosive internally

145
00:08:43,340 --> 00:08:48,660
to crush that ball of plutonium and spark off the explosion that

146
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:50,120
resulted in the detonation.

147
00:08:52,360 --> 00:08:55,020
Scientists determined that such complex technology

148
00:08:55,120 --> 00:08:57,260
required a test.

149
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:02,000
On July 16, 1945, the gadget was hoisted into position

150
00:09:02,100 --> 00:09:05,700
at what is now the White Sands Missile Range in New Mexico,

151
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:08,600
and a countdown into the nuclear age began.

152
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:13,920
Five, four, three, two, one.

153
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:27,360
We are now prepared to destroy more rapidly and completely

154
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:33,440
every productive enterprise the Japanese have in any city.

155
00:09:33,540 --> 00:09:37,000
On August 6, 1945, a gun-triggered fission

156
00:09:37,100 --> 00:09:41,180
bomb carried by a B-29 bomber called the Enola Gay fell

157
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:43,020
on the Japanese city of Hiroshima.

158
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:51,920
It detonated with an explosive force equal to 14,500

159
00:09:51,921 --> 00:09:56,920
tons of TNT. Then on August 9th,

160
00:09:56,921 --> 00:09:59,920
the US dropped an even more powerful implosion-triggered

161
00:09:59,921 --> 00:10:05,920
fission bomb on Nagasaki. It exploded with a force of 20,000

162
00:10:05,921 --> 00:10:10,920
tons of TNT. Though these superweapons ended

163
00:10:10,921 --> 00:10:14,920
World War II, the death and destruction left Los Alamos

164
00:10:14,921 --> 00:10:17,920
scientists deeply conflicted about the genie they let out

165
00:10:17,921 --> 00:10:21,920
of the bottle. I think the most important

166
00:10:21,921 --> 00:10:26,760
our work contributed to the safety of the world.

167
00:10:26,761 --> 00:10:31,520
And that was a good experience, but not in a way

168
00:10:31,521 --> 00:10:34,880
of big scientific discoveries, just in a way

169
00:10:34,881 --> 00:10:38,920
of outdoing diligently what had to be done.

170
00:10:38,921 --> 00:10:42,160
But there was no stopping nuclear technology.

171
00:10:42,161 --> 00:10:45,040
Physicists worked to miniaturize nuclear bombs,

172
00:10:45,041 --> 00:10:48,760
making possible a variety of delivery methods.

173
00:10:48,761 --> 00:10:51,280
The first 20 years that they were around,

174
00:10:51,281 --> 00:10:53,520
They were about the size of a tractor-trailer truck.

175
00:10:53,521 --> 00:10:55,580
They weighed 10 to 20,000 pounds.

176
00:10:55,581 --> 00:10:59,160
They took the world's largest airplane to deliver them,

177
00:10:59,161 --> 00:11:02,560
and that airplane could deliver one.

178
00:11:02,561 --> 00:11:05,660
In the aftermath, if we get into the 1950s,

179
00:11:05,661 --> 00:11:08,200
the diameter gets less than five feet.

180
00:11:08,201 --> 00:11:11,440
It can go down to eventually a six-inch artillery shell,

181
00:11:11,441 --> 00:11:13,140
which is quite remarkable.

182
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:19,840
After the war, many other methods

183
00:11:19,841 --> 00:11:24,840
from missiles to all sorts of naval weapons.

184
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:27,640
Weapons were not the only military use

185
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:29,980
for nuclear technology.

186
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:32,980
US Navy Captain Hyman Rickover pushed hard

187
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:35,780
to develop the first nuclear-powered war machine,

188
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:37,600
the Nautilus submarine.

189
00:11:37,700 --> 00:11:42,700
It was seaworthy by January 1954.

190
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:46,800
In December 1959, the Navy unveiled the USS George

191
00:11:46,801 --> 00:11:49,800
George Washington, the first of a five-boat class

192
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:52,740
of nuclear submarines.

193
00:11:52,840 --> 00:11:55,800
They were powered by a golf ball-sized chunk of uranium,

194
00:11:55,900 --> 00:12:00,240
and they carried nuclear weaponry as well, 16 Polaris missiles,

195
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:02,840
each topped with a 50-kiloton nuclear warhead.

196
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:09,260
After fission bombs, weapons designers developed the even

197
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:12,560
more destructive hydrogen bomb.

198
00:12:12,660 --> 00:12:15,460
Rather than splitting atoms as in a fission bomb,

199
00:12:15,461 --> 00:12:20,900
The hydrogen, or thermonuclear bomb, fuses atoms of hydrogen.

200
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,700
This creates an even more powerful chain reaction

201
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:26,900
than was possible in a fission bomb.

202
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,700
Believe it or not, the Russians even

203
00:12:29,800 --> 00:12:33,980
built a 100 megaton bomb at some point when they thought

204
00:12:34,080 --> 00:12:36,620
bigger was better.

205
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:40,720
A 100 megaton bomb is about 7,000 times more powerful

206
00:12:40,820 --> 00:12:44,220
than the bomb dropped on Hiroshima.

207
00:12:44,221 --> 00:12:46,760
As lethality increased, bomb designers

208
00:12:46,860 --> 00:12:50,960
wanted to make unauthorized detonations more difficult.

209
00:12:51,060 --> 00:12:53,760
This meant protecting the high explosive trigger,

210
00:12:53,860 --> 00:12:56,960
known as a sensitive HE pellet.

211
00:12:57,060 --> 00:13:00,060
This is a mechanical safe and armed detonator.

212
00:13:00,160 --> 00:13:02,280
You can see, if you look closely,

213
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:04,600
that a series of pushes and pulls

214
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:09,740
rotates this little wheel, takes the sensitive HE pellet,

215
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:13,820
and lines it up with this gun barrel on the back here.

216
00:13:13,821 --> 00:13:17,820
Well, only a very large number of secret coded pushes

217
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:19,720
and pulls can achieve that.

218
00:13:19,820 --> 00:13:21,820
Any other series, if you make one mistake,

219
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:24,360
and I actually have made one mistake now here,

220
00:13:24,460 --> 00:13:26,460
and it's locked up.

221
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:30,160
Detonators of this type cost as much as $50,000.

222
00:13:30,260 --> 00:13:32,780
But their Swiss watch tolerances are required

223
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:35,780
to be sure a nuclear warhead doesn't detonate until armed

224
00:13:35,880 --> 00:13:37,420
during a launch sequence.

225
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:38,420
Hands on keys.

226
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:39,520
Keys on my mark.

227
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:43,820
Three, two, one.

228
00:13:43,821 --> 00:13:48,820
Atomic weapons were and still are the last word in war,

229
00:13:48,920 --> 00:13:52,820
but nuclear technology has also created peacetime applications

230
00:13:52,920 --> 00:13:56,760
defended by friends and villainized by foes.

231
00:14:00,260 --> 00:14:03,680
The United States government maintains a stockpile

232
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:07,680
of more than 10,000 nuclear warheads.

233
00:14:07,780 --> 00:14:11,680
Nuclear tech will return on Modern Marvels.

234
00:14:13,820 --> 00:14:20,820
We now return to nuclear tech on Modern Marvels.

235
00:14:20,821 --> 00:14:27,820
In the 1950s, America envisioned a bright nuclear future,

236
00:14:27,821 --> 00:14:30,820
and the US military wanted its soldiers to be familiar with the

237
00:14:30,821 --> 00:14:34,820
new technology. Put on goggles or face

238
00:14:34,821 --> 00:14:38,820
away from the target area. Bomb away.

239
00:14:38,821 --> 00:14:44,820
Watching a bomb blast from across the Nevada desert may

240
00:14:44,821 --> 00:14:49,820
appear naive today, but in the 50s, such enthusiasm for all

241
00:14:49,821 --> 00:14:52,820
things nuclear went right to the top.

242
00:14:55,820 --> 00:14:58,820
President Dwight D. Eisenhower announced what was known as the

243
00:14:58,821 --> 00:15:01,820
Atoms for Peace Initiative in the United Nations General

244
00:15:01,821 --> 00:15:05,820
Assembly on December 18, 1953.

245
00:15:05,821 --> 00:15:13,820
The United States pledges before you to devote its entire heart and mind to find the way

246
00:15:13,821 --> 00:15:20,780
by which the miraculous inventiveness of man shall not be dedicated to his death, but

247
00:15:20,781 --> 00:15:22,520
consecrated to his life.

248
00:15:24,100 --> 00:15:29,580
In the early 1950s, President Eisenhower talked about atoms for peace.

249
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:35,740
And this was a program which spread nuclear technologies to countries around the world.

250
00:15:35,820 --> 00:15:38,460
I gave them research reactors.

251
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:41,460
Much of this is coming back to haunt us today.

252
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:43,060
Spreading the knowledge, of course,

253
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:49,360
has a dark side if they decide to pursue a military path.

254
00:15:49,460 --> 00:15:51,460
Many were worrying about the bomb,

255
00:15:51,560 --> 00:15:52,940
but some engineers and scientists

256
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:55,700
were also quite taken with the possibility

257
00:15:55,800 --> 00:15:59,240
of using atomic power to heat homes, fuel airplanes,

258
00:15:59,340 --> 00:16:02,180
and undertake massive public works projects.

259
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:05,420
Nuclear explosives may be the most efficient

260
00:16:05,421 --> 00:16:12,420
efficient way to excavate a sea level canal safely, quickly and for less cost than conventional means.

261
00:16:12,421 --> 00:16:19,420
It's called peaceful nuclear explosions and you'd put a string of them under the ground to detonate them all off and you'd have this huge

262
00:16:19,421 --> 00:16:28,420
either hole or canal and the Russians did a lot with that to try to redirect river flow in Siberia but it left a lot of

263
00:16:28,421 --> 00:16:30,277
radioactive debris around.

264
00:16:30,278 --> 00:16:35,420
We had on the books for maybe 30 years the Corps of Engineers of digging

265
00:16:35,421 --> 00:16:38,520
a new Panama Canal, either in Panama or Nicaragua,

266
00:16:38,521 --> 00:16:41,760
using nuclear weapons, we now realize that it's probably

267
00:16:41,761 --> 00:16:44,320
not the way to do it.

268
00:16:44,321 --> 00:16:49,560
In 1955, nuclear power was about to meet with success.

269
00:16:49,561 --> 00:16:52,100
On July 17th, a little after midnight,

270
00:16:52,101 --> 00:16:54,880
the world's first nuclear power reactor went online

271
00:16:54,881 --> 00:16:56,980
in a brief demonstration.

272
00:16:56,981 --> 00:17:01,480
It provided electricity to the little town of Arco, Idaho.

273
00:17:01,481 --> 00:17:03,520
Experts predicted that the mighty atom would make

274
00:17:03,521 --> 00:17:06,520
so cheaply that it might someday be free.

275
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:12,020
Today, 20% of America's electrical power

276
00:17:12,021 --> 00:17:15,360
is generated by nuclear reactors.

277
00:17:15,361 --> 00:17:17,860
A nuclear reactor is really a sophisticated version

278
00:17:17,861 --> 00:17:20,280
of a steam engine.

279
00:17:20,281 --> 00:17:23,280
Reactors use the heat energy given off by nuclear fission

280
00:17:23,281 --> 00:17:27,780
to heat water, creating steam, which is used to turn turbines

281
00:17:27,781 --> 00:17:30,280
that generate electricity.

282
00:17:30,281 --> 00:17:32,720
The uranium core where that fission takes place

283
00:17:32,721 --> 00:17:37,160
takes place is contained inside a reactor vessel.

284
00:17:37,260 --> 00:17:39,760
In the vessel are fuel rods of zirconium,

285
00:17:39,860 --> 00:17:43,540
filled with pellets of slightly enriched uranium.

286
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:47,260
Control rods made of boron carbide, which absorb neutrons,

287
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:52,600
are moved in to slow the reaction, moved out to speed it up.

288
00:17:52,700 --> 00:17:56,040
The bright prospects of nuclear power dimmed considerably.

289
00:17:56,140 --> 00:18:00,040
In the early morning hours of March 28, 1979,

290
00:18:00,041 --> 00:18:03,040
A nuclear power plant near Harrisburg, Pennsylvania,

291
00:18:03,041 --> 00:18:07,580
called Three Mile Island Unit 2, began a dangerous process

292
00:18:07,581 --> 00:18:09,380
of meltdown.

293
00:18:09,381 --> 00:18:13,780
So I was first apprised of this problem early yesterday.

294
00:18:13,781 --> 00:18:18,200
We have activated state health and environmental experts

295
00:18:18,201 --> 00:18:21,960
and called immediately upon technicians from the Nuclear

296
00:18:21,961 --> 00:18:25,240
Regulatory Commission, the United States Department of

297
00:18:25,241 --> 00:18:30,040
Energy, as well as other private sources.

298
00:18:30,041 --> 00:18:32,680
What happened at Three Mile Island and what a meltdown

299
00:18:32,780 --> 00:18:36,280
is is you lose this coolant water for a while.

300
00:18:36,380 --> 00:18:38,820
And even though maybe the fission reaction still

301
00:18:38,920 --> 00:18:43,220
isn't going on, it's so hot that it will melt the fuel rods.

302
00:18:43,320 --> 00:18:45,060
It will melt the whole casing.

303
00:18:45,160 --> 00:18:48,860
And it will melt through the bottom of the reactor.

304
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:51,660
Three Mile Island was a public relations disaster

305
00:18:51,760 --> 00:18:54,200
for the nuclear industry.

306
00:18:54,300 --> 00:18:56,300
Though limited radiation was released,

307
00:18:56,400 --> 00:18:58,700
the event induced widespread panic

308
00:18:58,701 --> 00:19:04,000
and a fear of all things nuclear among the general population.

309
00:19:04,100 --> 00:19:05,540
After the accident, the government

310
00:19:05,640 --> 00:19:08,940
initiated better safety training for reactor personnel

311
00:19:09,040 --> 00:19:12,180
and made design changes in control rooms.

312
00:19:12,280 --> 00:19:14,620
The way the control room was designed

313
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:18,120
didn't facilitate people responding to an accident.

314
00:19:18,220 --> 00:19:20,720
There were some controls that red meant something

315
00:19:20,820 --> 00:19:22,460
and green meant something.

316
00:19:22,560 --> 00:19:25,260
And then other controls were just meant the opposite.

317
00:19:25,261 --> 00:19:28,360
So it really wasn't designed for helping people

318
00:19:28,460 --> 00:19:30,200
in a crisis situation.

319
00:19:30,300 --> 00:19:34,300
But then, in April 1986, the public's worst fears

320
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:38,300
about nuclear reactors were confirmed.

321
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:42,180
In Ukraine, then a republic of the former Soviet Union,

322
00:19:42,280 --> 00:19:44,480
the Chernobyl nuclear reactor exploded

323
00:19:44,580 --> 00:19:47,780
during a routine test.

324
00:19:47,880 --> 00:19:52,260
The area of radioactive release was 1,700 times larger

325
00:19:52,261 --> 00:19:55,100
than that of Three Mile Island, with 200 times

326
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:58,840
the number of people exposed to the radioactive dispersal.

327
00:19:58,940 --> 00:20:01,840
30 people died, and 209 were treated

328
00:20:01,940 --> 00:20:04,680
for acute radiation exposure.

329
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:07,380
At Chernobyl, we did have a true catastrophe.

330
00:20:07,480 --> 00:20:11,340
And that, I think, really caused global doubts

331
00:20:11,440 --> 00:20:13,380
about nuclear power.

332
00:20:13,480 --> 00:20:17,020
It had a profound effect inside the Soviet Union at the time,

333
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:20,720
certainly on Gorbachev, and probably contributed

334
00:20:20,721 --> 00:20:25,160
contributed in its own way to the downfall of the Soviet Union.

335
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:27,920
Chernobyl was a harsh lesson for reactor designers

336
00:20:28,020 --> 00:20:29,860
and safety officials.

337
00:20:29,960 --> 00:20:31,460
But the problem of a core meltdown

338
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:34,560
wasn't the only challenge they faced.

339
00:20:34,660 --> 00:20:38,180
Even the routine transport of nuclear waste produced by reactors

340
00:20:38,280 --> 00:20:40,740
and other sources carries its own risks

341
00:20:40,840 --> 00:20:45,620
because of the waste's continuing radioactivity.

342
00:20:45,720 --> 00:20:48,440
Used reactor fuel rods remain hazardous for hundreds

343
00:20:48,441 --> 00:20:50,680
of thousands of years.

344
00:20:50,681 --> 00:20:52,540
Billions of gallons of nuclear waste

345
00:20:52,541 --> 00:20:54,680
from both military and commercial sources

346
00:20:54,681 --> 00:20:58,840
reside in underground facilities, much of it

347
00:20:58,841 --> 00:21:02,260
slowly leaking into groundwater supplies.

348
00:21:02,261 --> 00:21:05,160
Scientists debate actively the potential health effects

349
00:21:05,161 --> 00:21:07,660
of this contamination.

350
00:21:07,661 --> 00:21:09,820
Authorities are also concerned about security

351
00:21:09,821 --> 00:21:11,860
at nuclear power plants.

352
00:21:11,861 --> 00:21:13,900
They've taken steps to prevent the theft

353
00:21:13,901 --> 00:21:16,700
of radioactive materials.

354
00:21:16,701 --> 00:21:21,700
in the wrong hands could be used to make a dispersal device.

355
00:21:21,800 --> 00:21:23,640
In principle, I don't need a bomb

356
00:21:23,740 --> 00:21:25,440
to spread this radioactivity around.

357
00:21:25,540 --> 00:21:27,040
I can go on top of a tall building

358
00:21:27,140 --> 00:21:29,940
and just shake a powder over the side in a high wind.

359
00:21:30,040 --> 00:21:32,440
It's a way of just getting radioactivity out.

360
00:21:32,540 --> 00:21:35,820
Such a bomb or radiological attack

361
00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:37,560
would not necessarily kill anyone.

362
00:21:37,660 --> 00:21:43,060
It's largely an economic and psychological attack.

363
00:21:43,160 --> 00:21:45,360
Released in a crowded city or anywhere,

364
00:21:45,361 --> 00:21:48,760
masses of people gather, a so-called dirty bomb would

365
00:21:48,860 --> 00:21:53,160
spread terror and economic devastation.

366
00:21:53,260 --> 00:21:56,500
To address this new threat, scientists would need to develop

367
00:21:56,600 --> 00:21:59,800
new ways of detecting radioactive sources.

368
00:22:04,420 --> 00:22:10,160
In France, nearly 60 nuclear reactors supply almost 80% of

369
00:22:10,260 --> 00:22:13,360
the country's electricity.

370
00:22:13,361 --> 00:22:19,840
Nuclear Tech will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

371
00:22:25,800 --> 00:22:29,640
We now return to Nuclear Tech on Modern Marvels.

372
00:22:33,340 --> 00:22:38,140
Radiation is constantly with us. Some of it is harmful, some naturally occurring.

373
00:22:39,060 --> 00:22:42,340
Scientists have developed ways to detect radiation in all its forms.

374
00:22:42,341 --> 00:22:47,340
In 1928, Hans Geiger and a colleague developed the Geiger

375
00:22:47,341 --> 00:22:49,340
Mueller counter.

376
00:22:49,341 --> 00:22:52,340
A Geiger counter is a device for measuring the rate of

377
00:22:52,341 --> 00:22:54,340
radioactive decay.

378
00:22:54,341 --> 00:22:57,340
The heart of a Geiger counter is a gas-filled tube that

379
00:22:57,341 --> 00:22:59,340
conducts electricity.

380
00:22:59,341 --> 00:23:02,340
It becomes conductive when ionized by a particle emitted

381
00:23:02,341 --> 00:23:05,340
by a radioactive source.

382
00:23:05,341 --> 00:23:08,340
The charged gas completes an electrical circuit, and the

383
00:23:08,341 --> 00:23:11,340
Geiger counter amplifies the signal and displays it with a

384
00:23:11,341 --> 00:23:15,680
and displays it with a jumping needle and an audible click.

385
00:23:15,681 --> 00:23:17,840
The next generation of radiation detection

386
00:23:17,841 --> 00:23:20,840
is also a handheld unit, but is light years ahead

387
00:23:20,841 --> 00:23:22,460
of the old Geiger counter.

388
00:23:22,461 --> 00:23:24,880
It's called the Rad Scout.

389
00:23:24,881 --> 00:23:28,020
At the very front of the device is the high-period germanium

390
00:23:28,021 --> 00:23:28,920
crystal itself.

391
00:23:28,921 --> 00:23:32,100
That's what's measuring the gamma radiation, X radiation.

392
00:23:32,101 --> 00:23:34,360
That's inside the silver tube at the end,

393
00:23:34,361 --> 00:23:36,360
this gray foam we have on the prototype,

394
00:23:36,361 --> 00:23:38,240
simply to keep us from damaging it

395
00:23:38,241 --> 00:23:41,040
as we move it around the laboratory.

396
00:23:41,041 --> 00:23:43,540
The germanium crystals were first used for gamma ray

397
00:23:43,541 --> 00:23:46,740
detection in 1962.

398
00:23:46,741 --> 00:23:49,780
They work because each gamma ray has a signature wavelength,

399
00:23:49,781 --> 00:23:51,080
like a fingerprint.

400
00:23:51,081 --> 00:23:53,120
And the crystal can convert this fingerprint

401
00:23:53,121 --> 00:23:56,160
to electrical pulses.

402
00:23:56,161 --> 00:23:58,120
For the crystal to function properly,

403
00:23:58,121 --> 00:24:01,260
it must be kept at a temperature of minus 300 degrees

404
00:24:01,261 --> 00:24:03,900
Fahrenheit, using a portable refrigerator

405
00:24:03,901 --> 00:24:05,300
in the back of the unit.

406
00:24:05,301 --> 00:24:07,340
In the old days, we would keep those cold by that bottle

407
00:24:07,341 --> 00:24:08,460
of liquid nitrogen.

408
00:24:08,461 --> 00:24:11,040
Now we've got a refrigerator back here that keeps it cold.

409
00:24:11,041 --> 00:24:12,280
We're much more portable.

410
00:24:12,281 --> 00:24:14,740
We don't have to be tied to a supply of liquid nitrogen

411
00:24:14,741 --> 00:24:16,980
that would run out every day.

412
00:24:16,981 --> 00:24:19,680
Inside the Rad Scout, an onboard computer

413
00:24:19,681 --> 00:24:22,580
analyzes the data instantly.

414
00:24:22,581 --> 00:24:25,480
A non-scientist can use the device

415
00:24:25,481 --> 00:24:27,800
and identify what's causing the radiation

416
00:24:27,801 --> 00:24:29,400
and start the process of determining

417
00:24:29,401 --> 00:24:30,900
whether there's a real threat there

418
00:24:30,901 --> 00:24:34,500
or if it's a legitimate radioactive source.

419
00:24:34,501 --> 00:24:36,300
But the Rad Scout isn't the only way

420
00:24:36,301 --> 00:24:38,940
to detect a radiation threat.

421
00:24:38,941 --> 00:24:42,180
Scientists are putting nuclear technologies to practical use

422
00:24:42,280 --> 00:24:43,880
in the hope of protecting the public.

423
00:24:47,380 --> 00:24:49,980
At the Global Marine Terminal in New Jersey,

424
00:24:50,080 --> 00:24:52,180
there is a US Customs Inspection Station

425
00:24:52,280 --> 00:24:54,780
charged with examining every shipping container that

426
00:24:54,880 --> 00:24:58,400
is offloaded onto a waiting 18-wheeler.

427
00:24:58,500 --> 00:25:00,800
The containers hail from all over the world,

428
00:25:00,900 --> 00:25:04,700
and a surprising number of them carry radioactive materials.

429
00:25:04,800 --> 00:25:06,700
They pass through radiation portals

430
00:25:06,701 --> 00:25:10,400
that function like giant Geiger counters.

431
00:25:10,500 --> 00:25:12,400
On any given day at this particular terminal,

432
00:25:12,500 --> 00:25:15,740
we see about 500 containers on average, on the high side,

433
00:25:15,840 --> 00:25:18,940
600 a day that exit this facility.

434
00:25:19,040 --> 00:25:23,320
From that, we probably get about four or five radiation hits

435
00:25:23,420 --> 00:25:26,020
a day on average.

436
00:25:26,120 --> 00:25:27,180
Alert.

437
00:25:27,280 --> 00:25:28,020
Alert.

438
00:25:28,120 --> 00:25:29,020
Alert.

439
00:25:29,120 --> 00:25:31,820
When a hit occurs and the alarm sounds,

440
00:25:31,920 --> 00:25:35,320
customs officers direct the truck to a second radiation portal

441
00:25:35,321 --> 00:25:37,620
for additional screening.

442
00:25:37,720 --> 00:25:40,260
That's necessary because the portals are so sensitive,

443
00:25:40,360 --> 00:25:42,720
they pick up even low levels of radiation,

444
00:25:42,820 --> 00:25:44,460
some of which occur naturally.

445
00:25:44,560 --> 00:25:46,560
It's very common that we see radiation hits

446
00:25:46,660 --> 00:25:50,400
from things like ceramic tiles, stoneware, kitty litter.

447
00:25:50,500 --> 00:25:52,400
These type of things have naturally occurring

448
00:25:52,500 --> 00:25:53,800
radioactive elements.

449
00:25:53,900 --> 00:25:56,740
Low level, but it's good that our sensors can pick it up

450
00:25:56,840 --> 00:25:58,940
even at that low level.

451
00:25:59,040 --> 00:26:01,120
Using an automated targeting system,

452
00:26:01,220 --> 00:26:03,620
the custom station is equipped to compare the cargo

453
00:26:03,621 --> 00:26:07,620
in the container with a shipping manifest.

454
00:26:07,720 --> 00:26:10,720
Officers also inspect a visual chart of the radiation

455
00:26:10,820 --> 00:26:12,420
called a spectra.

456
00:26:12,520 --> 00:26:14,860
We will get a graph, as we see on the screen here,

457
00:26:14,960 --> 00:26:18,360
telling us exactly what the radiation source is.

458
00:26:18,460 --> 00:26:20,380
We marry up the information, the two pieces

459
00:26:20,480 --> 00:26:21,580
of electronic information.

460
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:24,780
It gives us a clearer picture of the risk and the threat

461
00:26:24,880 --> 00:26:27,180
assessment of all the containers.

462
00:26:27,280 --> 00:26:28,180
Here's the cab.

463
00:26:28,280 --> 00:26:32,880
Looks like it's right in the center, so right about there.

464
00:26:32,881 --> 00:26:35,620
Customs officers use a handheld radiation detector

465
00:26:35,720 --> 00:26:37,120
to take readings.

466
00:26:37,220 --> 00:26:39,080
And if the data are too weak to analyze

467
00:26:39,180 --> 00:26:41,220
or otherwise inconclusive, they'll

468
00:26:41,320 --> 00:26:42,920
crack the custom seal and get a visual.

469
00:26:46,060 --> 00:26:48,520
OK, this is giving me an unknown isotope.

470
00:26:48,620 --> 00:26:49,640
We're going to have to open the container.

471
00:26:52,740 --> 00:26:54,340
We got to pop it.

472
00:26:54,440 --> 00:26:57,040
Simple matter of taking a pair of bowl cutters, snapping it off.

473
00:26:59,780 --> 00:27:02,180
We record the seal, and then we put a custom seal on when

474
00:27:02,181 --> 00:27:03,180
when we're done.

475
00:27:09,780 --> 00:27:12,520
This particular container is manifested as stoneware,

476
00:27:12,620 --> 00:27:15,920
and it's some sort of concrete and granite material,

477
00:27:16,020 --> 00:27:20,940
and it's something that would naturally set off radiation.

478
00:27:21,040 --> 00:27:22,740
The technology of the portal monitor

479
00:27:22,840 --> 00:27:25,840
has its detractors.

480
00:27:25,940 --> 00:27:28,240
They've questioned whether stolen fission materials

481
00:27:28,340 --> 00:27:32,180
might escape detection by the portal system.

482
00:27:32,181 --> 00:27:36,780
While the portals can easily detect plutonium and dirty bomb

483
00:27:36,880 --> 00:27:40,320
materials, they're less effective in detecting highly enriched

484
00:27:40,420 --> 00:27:43,660
uranium, an isotope readily found at nuclear weapons

485
00:27:43,760 --> 00:27:44,660
stockpile facilities.

486
00:27:48,760 --> 00:27:51,840
If we want to solve the highly enriched uranium terrorist

487
00:27:51,940 --> 00:27:56,500
problem, you have to lock this material up at the source or

488
00:27:56,600 --> 00:27:58,500
eliminate it at the source.

489
00:27:58,501 --> 00:28:02,800
we can at least round up the material in the civil programs

490
00:28:02,900 --> 00:28:06,100
and greatly reduce the threat of terrorists

491
00:28:06,200 --> 00:28:09,240
using nuclear weapons.

492
00:28:09,340 --> 00:28:11,740
Smuggling nuclear materials across borders

493
00:28:11,840 --> 00:28:14,520
remains a troubling problem, and scientists

494
00:28:14,620 --> 00:28:16,580
are working to address it with new forms

495
00:28:16,680 --> 00:28:19,060
of nuclear technology.

496
00:28:19,160 --> 00:28:21,020
They're worried about people possibly stealing

497
00:28:21,120 --> 00:28:23,200
nuclear material or nuclear weapons

498
00:28:23,300 --> 00:28:25,120
and bringing them into the United States,

499
00:28:25,220 --> 00:28:28,500
where you could obviously do a huge amount of damage.

500
00:28:28,501 --> 00:28:31,340
Scientists are developing a handheld unit that could be

501
00:28:31,440 --> 00:28:34,240
distributed to emergency workers.

502
00:28:34,340 --> 00:28:36,740
So this is a special cell phone that's

503
00:28:36,840 --> 00:28:39,040
also a personal digital assistant.

504
00:28:39,140 --> 00:28:41,840
And we strapped on the back here some electronics,

505
00:28:41,940 --> 00:28:44,540
including a global positioning satellite receiver,

506
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:45,960
GPS receiver.

507
00:28:46,060 --> 00:28:49,760
And these detectors fit in here like this.

508
00:28:49,860 --> 00:28:51,660
The chip that fits in this prototype

509
00:28:51,760 --> 00:28:56,000
was originally made to detect gamma rays in space.

510
00:28:56,100 --> 00:28:58,500
To reach the upper atmosphere, the sensors have

511
00:28:58,501 --> 00:28:59,500
lightweight.

512
00:28:59,600 --> 00:29:02,140
They were also relatively cheap.

513
00:29:02,240 --> 00:29:05,280
Researchers later realized that those qualities made the sensors

514
00:29:05,380 --> 00:29:08,480
the perfect candidates for detecting radioactive sources

515
00:29:08,580 --> 00:29:10,820
in urban environments.

516
00:29:10,920 --> 00:29:12,740
Individually, they may not have a whole lot of power,

517
00:29:12,840 --> 00:29:15,080
but if we have a lot of them, I'm talking about hundreds

518
00:29:15,180 --> 00:29:17,820
or even thousands of these things in one given city

519
00:29:17,920 --> 00:29:20,660
region, for example, they all are constantly measuring

520
00:29:20,760 --> 00:29:22,760
the radioactive, they're constantly making measurements,

521
00:29:22,860 --> 00:29:24,760
and they send all those signals back

522
00:29:24,860 --> 00:29:27,700
to a central processing system that's

523
00:29:27,701 --> 00:29:29,200
It's going to look at all those things

524
00:29:29,300 --> 00:29:32,100
and look at them all together as if they were one big detector

525
00:29:32,200 --> 00:29:33,840
that's spread out.

526
00:29:33,940 --> 00:29:38,040
And therefore, we believe, have a very sensitive system.

527
00:29:38,140 --> 00:29:40,240
Armed with this passively gathered data,

528
00:29:40,340 --> 00:29:43,640
analysts could create a dynamic map of an entire city.

529
00:29:43,740 --> 00:29:45,060
There's a bit of randomness to it, too.

530
00:29:45,160 --> 00:29:46,160
It's just another thing.

531
00:29:46,260 --> 00:29:47,760
If I have one big detector, well,

532
00:29:47,860 --> 00:29:49,160
it's going to be pretty obvious where that is.

533
00:29:49,260 --> 00:29:50,760
It might be easy to go around it.

534
00:29:50,860 --> 00:29:52,300
But if these things are everywhere,

535
00:29:52,400 --> 00:29:56,200
it's hard to go around because you don't know where they are.

536
00:29:56,201 --> 00:29:59,000
The result is a smart network that is constantly updating

537
00:29:59,100 --> 00:30:02,700
authorities on radiation conditions.

538
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:06,940
But radiation can also be used productively at safe levels,

539
00:30:07,040 --> 00:30:10,140
at home, at work, and in medicine.

540
00:30:10,240 --> 00:30:14,060
The power of the atom is getting up close and personal.

541
00:30:18,960 --> 00:30:20,860
Common materials that are radioactive

542
00:30:20,960 --> 00:30:23,700
include concrete, which contains uranium

543
00:30:23,701 --> 00:30:27,300
in a concentration of 31 parts per million.

544
00:30:27,400 --> 00:30:30,000
Nuclear Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

545
00:30:38,040 --> 00:30:41,060
We now return to Nuclear Tech on Modern Marvels.

546
00:30:44,160 --> 00:30:46,560
This looks like an ordinary warehouse,

547
00:30:46,660 --> 00:30:50,260
but actually, it's a hotbed of nuclear tech.

548
00:30:50,360 --> 00:30:53,460
In a secure concrete chamber with walls 6 and 1 1

549
00:30:53,461 --> 00:30:57,260
thick is a deadly source of radioactive cobalt,

550
00:30:57,360 --> 00:30:58,460
glowing eerily blue.

551
00:31:02,360 --> 00:31:05,300
These medical supplies, cosmetics, and spices

552
00:31:05,400 --> 00:31:09,100
are about to receive a blast of gamma photons.

553
00:31:09,200 --> 00:31:11,220
They're being irradiated to reduce

554
00:31:11,320 --> 00:31:14,020
pathogenic organisms, E. coli, salmonella,

555
00:31:14,120 --> 00:31:16,020
or just general spoilage organisms,

556
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:18,920
yeast and molds that can contaminate subsequent products

557
00:31:19,020 --> 00:31:21,320
in the manufacturing process.

558
00:31:21,321 --> 00:31:22,980
In some cases, it's sterilization,

559
00:31:23,080 --> 00:31:26,320
and in some cases, it's just microbial decontamination

560
00:31:26,420 --> 00:31:29,260
or sanitization.

561
00:31:29,360 --> 00:31:32,200
The products are sanitized, but they aren't radioactive

562
00:31:32,300 --> 00:31:34,300
after the process.

563
00:31:34,400 --> 00:31:36,540
Since radiation penetrates packaging,

564
00:31:36,640 --> 00:31:39,180
the goods to be treated are loaded right off their shipping

565
00:31:39,280 --> 00:31:44,080
pallets into metal carriers, called totes.

566
00:31:44,180 --> 00:31:46,320
The amount of radiation the products receive

567
00:31:46,420 --> 00:31:49,540
is precisely controlled.

568
00:31:49,541 --> 00:31:51,500
Because if you get too high a dose,

569
00:31:51,600 --> 00:31:52,940
then you will either damage the product,

570
00:31:53,040 --> 00:31:55,080
some plastics degrade with radiation.

571
00:31:55,180 --> 00:31:58,180
In food irradiation, you will get some off flavors

572
00:31:58,280 --> 00:32:01,980
from lipids, fats in the meat if it goes too high a dose,

573
00:32:02,080 --> 00:32:03,980
and that sort of thing.

574
00:32:04,080 --> 00:32:06,020
An overhead track system guides the totes

575
00:32:06,120 --> 00:32:08,220
into the radiation cell and rotates them

576
00:32:08,320 --> 00:32:09,660
for even radiation exposure.

577
00:32:12,800 --> 00:32:15,640
Small radiation sensors, called dosimeters,

578
00:32:15,740 --> 00:32:17,640
monitor the dose each tote receives

579
00:32:17,641 --> 00:32:20,940
when it enters the radiation cell.

580
00:32:21,040 --> 00:32:23,380
With lethal levels inside the chamber,

581
00:32:23,480 --> 00:32:27,880
there's a warning system to prevent accidental access

582
00:32:31,980 --> 00:32:34,920
and a concrete maze designed to absorb the energy

583
00:32:35,020 --> 00:32:36,320
of the gamma radiation.

584
00:32:38,900 --> 00:32:42,840
It will be going down what we refer to as a maze.

585
00:32:42,940 --> 00:32:45,900
The gamma radiation, as it's emitted from the cobalt sources,

586
00:32:45,901 --> 00:32:49,700
sources, it bounces off the walls.

587
00:32:49,800 --> 00:32:52,740
This is on the order of about a factor of 1,000

588
00:32:52,840 --> 00:32:57,280
in its intensity for each bounce that it goes through.

589
00:32:57,380 --> 00:32:59,720
Inside the cell, the totes are shuffled

590
00:32:59,820 --> 00:33:02,660
by the overhead track system to keep radiation levels

591
00:33:02,760 --> 00:33:06,820
consistent during a dose period, ranging from 6 to 12 hours.

592
00:33:09,620 --> 00:33:11,660
The powerful dose of nuclear radiation

593
00:33:11,760 --> 00:33:14,600
sanitizes the product by interfering with the DNA

594
00:33:14,601 --> 00:33:19,840
process of any offending bacterium or other pesky organisms.

595
00:33:19,940 --> 00:33:23,000
That is, if it doesn't kill them instantly.

596
00:33:23,100 --> 00:33:26,540
The cobalt source is formed into slugs that are fitted into a

597
00:33:26,640 --> 00:33:29,740
metal rod, like this model.

598
00:33:29,840 --> 00:33:33,360
Cobalt is used because it is a high-yield, high-energy source

599
00:33:33,460 --> 00:33:35,360
of gamma radiation.

600
00:33:35,460 --> 00:33:39,660
While in use, the source is raised from a pool of water.

601
00:33:39,760 --> 00:33:42,360
But when humans must brave the cell for maintenance

602
00:33:42,361 --> 00:33:43,860
maintenance inspections.

603
00:33:43,960 --> 00:33:46,360
The source is lowered into its liquid radiation barrier.

604
00:33:49,460 --> 00:33:52,400
The source racks at this time are stored in the water pool,

605
00:33:52,500 --> 00:33:53,600
in the storage pool.

606
00:33:53,700 --> 00:33:56,300
You can see the blue glow from the cobalt,

607
00:33:56,400 --> 00:33:58,200
the radioactive decay of the cobalt.

608
00:33:58,300 --> 00:34:01,520
And we have somewhere around 10 to 12 feet of water

609
00:34:01,620 --> 00:34:05,120
above the source rack in this storage position.

610
00:34:05,220 --> 00:34:07,360
And you can see from the radiation survey meter

611
00:34:07,460 --> 00:34:09,560
is that we're picking up no radiation level

612
00:34:09,660 --> 00:34:12,360
or very little radiation levels at the top of the water.

613
00:34:12,361 --> 00:34:14,560
the pool with the sources in the stored position.

614
00:34:18,060 --> 00:34:20,300
Before shipping, the products are shrink-wrapped

615
00:34:20,301 --> 00:34:22,800
and labeled as gamma ray processed,

616
00:34:22,801 --> 00:34:27,040
free of microorganisms that could cause spoilage.

617
00:34:27,041 --> 00:34:29,000
But the public doesn't only come in contact

618
00:34:29,001 --> 00:34:31,840
with nuclear technology at the supermarket.

619
00:34:31,841 --> 00:34:35,740
It is used in other everyday applications.

620
00:34:35,741 --> 00:34:38,460
Costing about $10, a home test kit

621
00:34:38,461 --> 00:34:42,460
detects radon, a naturally occurring radioactive gas.

622
00:34:42,560 --> 00:34:44,560
It can accumulate in closed basements

623
00:34:44,660 --> 00:34:47,160
and cause lung cancer.

624
00:34:47,260 --> 00:34:50,100
For more nuclear tech at home, look no further than a common

625
00:34:50,200 --> 00:34:51,700
smoke detector.

626
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:55,140
Smoke detectors contain a small amount

627
00:34:55,240 --> 00:34:59,780
of radioactive material just to ionize the air to help detect

628
00:34:59,880 --> 00:35:02,320
smoke particles.

629
00:35:02,420 --> 00:35:04,560
Many home smoke detectors use an isotope

630
00:35:04,660 --> 00:35:07,820
called americium-241.

631
00:35:07,821 --> 00:35:11,020
The isotope emits alpha particles that ionize the air,

632
00:35:11,120 --> 00:35:13,460
allowing a current to pass between two electrodes

633
00:35:13,560 --> 00:35:15,060
in the device.

634
00:35:15,160 --> 00:35:17,560
If smoke enters the space between the electrodes,

635
00:35:17,660 --> 00:35:20,760
it absorbs the alpha particles, interrupts the current,

636
00:35:20,860 --> 00:35:23,060
and sets off the alarm.

637
00:35:23,160 --> 00:35:25,480
The isotope is embedded in a laminate thick enough

638
00:35:25,580 --> 00:35:27,980
to safely contain the radioactive material,

639
00:35:28,080 --> 00:35:32,080
but thin enough to allow the alpha particles to pass through.

640
00:35:32,180 --> 00:35:35,220
The isotope is shielded, so it's safe to handle a smoke

641
00:35:35,221 --> 00:35:38,020
and safe to dispose of in landfill,

642
00:35:38,120 --> 00:35:41,360
but it shouldn't be incinerated.

643
00:35:41,460 --> 00:35:43,300
This gamma watch would surely alert you

644
00:35:43,400 --> 00:35:46,600
to the radioactivity present in a smoke detector,

645
00:35:46,700 --> 00:35:50,260
and also to that found in welding rods.

646
00:35:50,360 --> 00:35:52,440
When they are placed close to the gamma watch,

647
00:35:52,540 --> 00:35:54,800
it beeps, displays the dose rate,

648
00:35:54,900 --> 00:35:58,080
and a cumulative dose rate over time.

649
00:35:58,180 --> 00:36:00,980
The cost for radiation peace of mind on your wrist?

650
00:36:01,080 --> 00:36:03,140
$500.

651
00:36:03,141 --> 00:36:05,940
But timekeeping itself has advanced thanks to nuclear

652
00:36:06,040 --> 00:36:09,340
technology, reaching amazing levels of accuracy

653
00:36:09,440 --> 00:36:12,880
with the development of atomic clocks.

654
00:36:12,980 --> 00:36:15,480
The difference between a standard clock in your home

655
00:36:15,580 --> 00:36:18,480
and an atomic clock is the nature of the oscillation

656
00:36:18,580 --> 00:36:20,600
that measures time.

657
00:36:20,700 --> 00:36:23,800
In a conventional clock, a balance wheel driven by a spring

658
00:36:23,900 --> 00:36:25,800
clicks off the seconds.

659
00:36:25,900 --> 00:36:28,540
In an atomic clock, the oscillation frequencies

660
00:36:28,640 --> 00:36:32,040
among atomic particles are a kind of electrostatic balance

661
00:36:32,041 --> 00:36:36,040
balance wheel, keeping the beat in their subatomic way.

662
00:36:36,140 --> 00:36:38,440
That's at such a high frequency that they're

663
00:36:38,540 --> 00:36:43,680
able to time that signal very accurately so it's accurate to,

664
00:36:43,780 --> 00:36:46,280
you know, one second in 8,000 years.

665
00:36:46,380 --> 00:36:50,400
Very crucial to our system of satellites called a GPS system.

666
00:36:50,500 --> 00:36:54,200
Those signals have to be synchronized in all the

667
00:36:54,300 --> 00:36:57,300
satellites.

668
00:36:57,400 --> 00:36:59,740
For about $300, you can buy your own version

669
00:36:59,741 --> 00:37:02,480
of an atomic clock that syncs up with a central system

670
00:37:02,580 --> 00:37:05,780
via radio signals.

671
00:37:05,880 --> 00:37:08,880
Nuclear technology is also used to map the human body

672
00:37:08,980 --> 00:37:09,880
from the inside.

673
00:37:09,980 --> 00:37:13,580
Magnetic resonance imaging involves signals coming

674
00:37:13,680 --> 00:37:16,800
from the proton in a water molecule.

675
00:37:16,900 --> 00:37:20,020
It used to be called, in the scientific realm,

676
00:37:20,120 --> 00:37:22,600
nuclear magnetic resonance.

677
00:37:22,700 --> 00:37:24,840
But it had that nuclear term on the front.

678
00:37:24,940 --> 00:37:27,240
So when you use it in a medical field,

679
00:37:27,340 --> 00:37:29,740
you sort of get rid of it and call it magnetic resonance.

680
00:37:29,741 --> 00:37:34,740
and that's had an enormous impact on diagnostic techniques.

681
00:37:34,840 --> 00:37:37,540
Magnetic resonance is not radioactive,

682
00:37:37,640 --> 00:37:40,780
but nuclear medicine has also developed the PET scan,

683
00:37:40,880 --> 00:37:44,180
which uses radioactive materials.

684
00:37:44,280 --> 00:37:47,400
PET, short for positron emission topography,

685
00:37:47,500 --> 00:37:50,400
generates images of the body by tracing small amounts

686
00:37:50,500 --> 00:37:53,600
of radioactive materials that a doctor injects

687
00:37:53,700 --> 00:37:57,100
into a patient's bloodstream.

688
00:37:57,101 --> 00:37:59,740
Researchers are continually developing new medical

689
00:37:59,840 --> 00:38:02,200
applications for nuclear technology.

690
00:38:02,300 --> 00:38:04,440
One of the newest nuclear cancer treatments

691
00:38:04,540 --> 00:38:07,840
involves a method known as brachytherapy.

692
00:38:07,940 --> 00:38:10,780
Oncologists place a dose of radiation directly

693
00:38:10,880 --> 00:38:15,120
into a tumor, either using radioactive seeds, like these,

694
00:38:15,220 --> 00:38:18,760
or by introducing the dose with a catheter.

695
00:38:18,860 --> 00:38:20,960
But while these feats may be impressive,

696
00:38:21,060 --> 00:38:27,100
if some nuclear dreams are right, the best is yet to come.

697
00:38:27,101 --> 00:38:31,100
Radiation cells are sometimes used to help librarians

698
00:38:31,200 --> 00:38:34,740
kill mold on books damaged by water.

699
00:38:34,840 --> 00:38:38,840
Nuclear Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

700
00:38:44,860 --> 00:38:48,760
We now return to Nuclear Tech on Modern Marvels.

701
00:38:50,660 --> 00:38:52,660
Scientists are building a device

702
00:38:52,760 --> 00:38:56,160
that might produce energy for our entire civilization.

703
00:38:56,161 --> 00:38:58,400
It would generate fusion power.

704
00:38:58,500 --> 00:39:02,300
And for some, that's the holy grail of atomic energy.

705
00:39:02,400 --> 00:39:06,400
Part of the reason for that is it has proved to be so hard.

706
00:39:06,500 --> 00:39:11,300
It's one of the grand scientific challenges of our age,

707
00:39:11,400 --> 00:39:15,880
and people have been trying to get this for more than 50

708
00:39:15,980 --> 00:39:18,520
years without success.

709
00:39:18,620 --> 00:39:21,320
Fusion power is highly sought after because it produces

710
00:39:21,420 --> 00:39:23,920
no radioactive waste.

711
00:39:23,921 --> 00:39:26,820
The byproduct of a fusion reaction is benign.

712
00:39:26,920 --> 00:39:30,720
It uses hydrogen isotopes, and eventually,

713
00:39:30,820 --> 00:39:33,560
if we design the type of reactor that we would like,

714
00:39:33,660 --> 00:39:38,960
it would just generate helium, which is not radioactive.

715
00:39:39,060 --> 00:39:41,580
A fusion reaction fuses atoms

716
00:39:41,680 --> 00:39:44,680
and is the same process that gives life to stars

717
00:39:44,780 --> 00:39:48,380
and powers hydrogen bombs.

718
00:39:48,480 --> 00:39:52,320
In the fusion process, light atoms of two hydrogen isotopes,

719
00:39:52,321 --> 00:39:56,260
isotopes, deuterium and tritium, are fused together at high

720
00:39:56,360 --> 00:39:59,060
temperature with an accompanying release of energy.

721
00:39:59,160 --> 00:40:02,400
The problem is, in order to get the two nuclei together,

722
00:40:02,500 --> 00:40:06,340
you have to put them under extreme temperature and pressure

723
00:40:06,440 --> 00:40:09,800
to force them together because they're positively charged

724
00:40:09,900 --> 00:40:13,580
objects and the electric force tends to repel them.

725
00:40:13,680 --> 00:40:16,720
At the Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory, a US

726
00:40:16,820 --> 00:40:20,740
Department of Energy facility, researchers explore fusion in a

727
00:40:20,741 --> 00:40:25,240
called a torus. A fusion reaction can only occur

728
00:40:25,340 --> 00:40:30,580
inside a plasma of gases. The torus uses strong magnetic fields

729
00:40:30,680 --> 00:40:34,380
to squeeze the plasma. This method of achieving fusion

730
00:40:34,480 --> 00:40:38,900
is called magnetic confinement. At Lawrence Livermore

731
00:40:39,000 --> 00:40:41,200
National Laboratories, the Department of Defense

732
00:40:41,300 --> 00:40:44,000
is studying fusion for its weapons applications

733
00:40:44,100 --> 00:40:48,800
and to develop quality control tests for nuclear warheads.

734
00:40:48,801 --> 00:40:50,600
Though the primary intent of this project

735
00:40:50,700 --> 00:40:52,900
is weapons research, a spinoff may

736
00:40:53,000 --> 00:40:57,300
be the creation of fusion power for peaceful uses.

737
00:40:57,400 --> 00:41:00,240
Researchers at Livermore plan to use powerful lasers

738
00:41:00,340 --> 00:41:03,740
to compress the plasma rather than magnetic energy.

739
00:41:03,840 --> 00:41:07,560
This is the target chamber for the National Ignition

740
00:41:07,660 --> 00:41:11,460
Facility, a very large laser facility that

741
00:41:11,560 --> 00:41:16,160
was designed to get fusion in the laboratory.

742
00:41:16,260 --> 00:41:18,360
This facility has cost more than $300

743
00:41:18,361 --> 00:41:22,800
$3.5 billion so far.

744
00:41:22,900 --> 00:41:25,000
It boasts a NASA-style control room,

745
00:41:25,100 --> 00:41:28,700
where technicians will guide the energy of 192 lasers

746
00:41:28,800 --> 00:41:30,900
through an amplification and filtering process.

747
00:41:33,900 --> 00:41:36,520
The lasers focus on a minuscule target of tritium

748
00:41:36,620 --> 00:41:37,620
and deuterium.

749
00:41:37,720 --> 00:41:40,820
And the way it works is sort of like the kind of things

750
00:41:40,920 --> 00:41:43,720
that you did as a kid with a lens.

751
00:41:43,820 --> 00:41:47,360
If you take a lens and focus sunlight onto a leaf,

752
00:41:47,361 --> 00:41:48,700
you can start a fire.

753
00:41:48,800 --> 00:41:50,100
And it's because what you're doing

754
00:41:50,200 --> 00:41:53,560
is you're concentrating the energy of the sun

755
00:41:53,660 --> 00:41:54,900
into a very small spot.

756
00:41:55,000 --> 00:41:58,500
And when you do that, you raise the temperature significantly.

757
00:42:03,300 --> 00:42:05,120
But scientists believe there are other ways

758
00:42:05,220 --> 00:42:06,720
to create fusion.

759
00:42:06,820 --> 00:42:08,820
At another weapons research facility,

760
00:42:08,920 --> 00:42:11,820
Sandia National Laboratories, scientists

761
00:42:11,920 --> 00:42:13,920
plan to use a powerful electrical pulse

762
00:42:14,020 --> 00:42:16,860
to compress plasma and foster fusion.

763
00:42:16,861 --> 00:42:19,820
This is called the inertial confinement method.

764
00:42:19,821 --> 00:42:23,260
At Sandia, the approach in inertial confinement fusion

765
00:42:23,261 --> 00:42:26,460
is to take the raw electrical power, we actually take off

766
00:42:26,461 --> 00:42:30,300
the power grid, we store that in a capacitor for about a

767
00:42:30,301 --> 00:42:33,500
minute and discharge it in a millionth of a second.

768
00:42:33,501 --> 00:42:37,740
That electrical energy then, we create a 50 trillion watt pulse

769
00:42:37,741 --> 00:42:41,340
of electrical energy going to the center of this 100 foot

770
00:42:41,341 --> 00:42:42,860
diameter machine.

771
00:42:42,861 --> 00:42:46,360
The Z machine takes up a room about the size

772
00:42:46,460 --> 00:42:48,260
of a high school gym.

773
00:42:48,360 --> 00:42:50,860
It's arranged in a wheel with spokes

774
00:42:50,960 --> 00:42:53,100
to guide a high-powered electrical pulse

775
00:42:53,200 --> 00:42:55,000
toward a tiny target.

776
00:42:55,100 --> 00:42:57,100
At the very center, at the very hub,

777
00:42:57,200 --> 00:43:00,020
we combine all the electrical current

778
00:43:00,120 --> 00:43:02,920
into a very small center section.

779
00:43:03,020 --> 00:43:06,620
When it reaches the center, the voltage is approximately 3

780
00:43:06,720 --> 00:43:11,620
million volts, and the current is 20 million amperes.

781
00:43:11,621 --> 00:43:14,380
When the current associated with that energy

782
00:43:14,460 --> 00:43:16,200
gets to the center of the machine,

783
00:43:16,260 --> 00:43:18,420
it implodes a very light conductor

784
00:43:18,500 --> 00:43:23,200
made of 300 thin wires, each thinner than a human hair.

785
00:43:23,260 --> 00:43:24,360
And then all of a sudden, you're

